<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sámi people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sami, Saami</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sámi are the indigenous people of the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s Kola Peninsula, a region they call Sápmi. Traditionally they lived by fishing, hunting, trapping and later reindeer herding, and they speak several related Uralic languages. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> they paid tribute and taxes to Norse and later Scandinavian and Russian rulers, and they now have their own parliaments in Norway, Sweden and Finland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sami-people.jpg" alt="Sámi people" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jeltz · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sami_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
